      "heading": "Step 9 (Experimental): Entertainment edit",
      "prompt": "You are the final editorial pass on a HotelTechReport YouTube product review.\n\nThe script is already factually correct, structurally sound, and aligned with our editorial requirements. DO NOT materially change the product analysis, claims, evidence, timestamps, pricing, case studies, reviewer quotes, or buying recommendations.\n\nYour job is to make the video substantially more watchable.\n\nThink like an elite YouTube editor working with a smart industry expert, not a marketer.\n\nThe problem:  \nOur reviews follow a consistent structure, which is intentional, but they can start to feel mechanical, repetitive, and overly templated.\n\nYour goal:  \nPreserve the underlying review format while making THIS PARTICULAR VIDEO feel like its own story.\n\nEDIT FOR:\n\n1. A stronger opening  \nThe first 20–30 seconds should create a reason to keep watching.\n\nDo not use generic hooks like:  \n- “If you’re looking for…”  \n- “In today’s video…”  \n- “Let’s dive in.”  \n- “But is it worth it?”  \n- “Here’s everything you need to know.”\n\nInstead, lead with the most interesting insight, tension, surprising feature, customer result, limitation, or buying question specific to this product.\n\n2. Curiosity throughout  \nLook for places where information can be sequenced more intelligently.\n\nOccasionally establish an unanswered question or tease an important finding that gets resolved shortly afterward.\n\nDo NOT manufacture suspense or hide useful information simply for retention.\n\n3. Pattern interruption  \nIdentify stretches where the script falls into a repetitive rhythm:  \n“Feature → explanation → benefit.”  \n“Another feature → explanation → benefit.”\n\nVary the presentation.\n\nFor example:  \n- lead with an operational problem  \n- make an observation from the demo  \n- compare something to how hotels normally handle it  \n- introduce a reviewer’s experience  \n- briefly give the verdict before explaining why  \n- call out something unexpectedly good or weak  \n- explain who would actually care about the feature\n\nDo not use the same device repeatedly.\n\n4. Better transitions  \nRemove mechanical section transitions such as:  \n“Now let’s look at…”  \n“Next up…”  \n“Moving on to…”  \n“Another key feature is…”\n\nTransitions should feel like one thought naturally creates the next.\n\nWhenever possible, connect sections through cause and effect, a buying question, or an observation.\n\n5. Sentence rhythm  \nMake the narration sound spoken rather than written.\n\nMix short punchy sentences with longer explanatory ones.\n\nBreak up paragraphs that have identical sentence lengths or structures.\n\nRemove unnecessary setup sentences.\n\n6. Personality and point of view  \nAllow the narrator to make intelligent observations.\n\nExamples of the TYPE of commentary we want:  \n- why something stood out  \n- why a hotel operator would care  \n- something that looks minor but has meaningful operational implications  \n- something impressive that competitors often struggle with  \n- something the vendor emphasizes that buyers should scrutinize  \n- a limitation worth knowing before purchasing\n\nThese observations must be supported by the evidence already in the script.\n\nDo not invent opinions simply to create personality.\n\n7. Specificity over generic praise  \nReplace generic statements such as:  \n“This is a powerful feature”  \n“This can save hotels time”  \n“This makes things easier”\n\nwith the actual operational implication whenever the script provides enough information to do so.\n\n8. Strategic callbacks  \nWhere natural, reference something mentioned earlier in the video so the script feels connected rather than modular.\n\nDo this sparingly.\n\n9. Section openings  \nEach major section should not sound like every other HotelTechReport review.\n\nRewrite section openings so they are specific to the story of this product.\n\nDo not change the required section order.\n\n10. Ruthlessly eliminate “YouTube AI voice”  \nRemove:  \n- fake enthusiasm  \n- excessive rhetorical questions  \n- dramatic one-line paragraphs  \n- clickbait  \n- consultant language  \n- corporate filler  \n- false dichotomies  \n- “Here’s the thing”  \n- “And that’s where X comes in”  \n- “But what really sets X apart…”  \n- “This isn’t just X, it’s Y”  \n- “At the end of the day”  \n- “Game changer”  \n- unnecessary em dashes  \n- repetitive three-item rhetorical lists  \n- obvious setup/payoff formulas used repeatedly\n\nIMPORTANT CONSTRAINT:\n\nEntertainment value should come from:  \nspecificity,  \ninsight,  \npacing,  \ncuriosity,  \ncontrast,  \ngood storytelling,  \nand strong editorial judgment.\n\nIt should NOT come from making the host sound louder, more promotional, more dramatic, or less credible.\n\nHotel executives should still feel like they are getting analysis from a knowledgeable industry insider.\n\nPRESERVE:  \n- all factual claims  \n- HotelTechReport data  \n- review quotes  \n- case-study results  \n- demo timestamps  \n- pricing information  \n- product limitations  \n- editorial independence  \n- required section order  \n- all important buying guidance\n\nYou may shorten repetitive material, but do not remove substantive information.\n\nFINAL TEST:\n\nRead the script as if you had watched five other HotelTechReport reviews immediately before this one.\n\nAny sentence, transition, hook, or structure that feels copied from a template should be rewritten so it feels native to THIS product.\n\nThen read it as a YouTube viewer.\n\nEvery 30–60 seconds, ask:  \n“Has the viewer just learned something interesting, gotten a new question answered, encountered a new idea, or had their attention reset?”\n\nIf not, improve that stretch without adding fluff.\n\nOUTPUT:  \nReturn the complete revised teleprompter-ready script.\n\nThen add a short section called “Entertainment edits made” with the 5–10 most meaningful changes you made and why they improve retention."
